{
"title": "Multi-spectral Observations of Lunar Occultations: I. Resolving The Dust Shell Around AFGL 5440",
"authors": "Paul M. Harvey and Andrew Oldag",
"field_subfield": "Astrophysics: circumstellar mass loss and dust shells around AGB stars",
"type_of_paper": "Observational + modeling study (single lunar occultation event)",
"executive_summary": "This paper presents high-speed, multi-wavelength (1–4 µm) spectrophotometric observations of a lunar occultation of the carbon-rich AGB star AFGL 5440, obtained with the pMIRAS instrument on the McDonald Observatory 2.7-m telescope on 28 Aug 2001. The authors exploit the milli-arcsecond angular resolution of lunar occultations to simultaneously sample the Fresnel fringe pattern of the central star and the thermal emission from the warmest dust in the circumstellar shell. They first show that a single-size uniform-disk model cannot reproduce the observed light curves across the full wavelength range, motivating a full radiative-transfer treatment with the DUSTY code. By fitting both the occultation fringes and the spectral energy distribution (SED), they constrain the inner dust-shell temperature to Tmax = 950 K ± 50 K, the 5-µm optical depth to 0.5 ± 0.1, and a radial density gradient of ρ ∝ r−2 (consistent with a radiatively driven hydrodynamic outflow). The best-fitting dust composition requires comparable amounts of amorphous carbon and graphite plus a small amount of SiC. A notable tension arises because the models cannot reproduce the IRAS 60-µm flux without invoking substantial graphite or a substantially higher past mass-loss rate. The study thus resolves the stellar photosphere (angular diameter ~3 mas) from the inner dust-shell edge (angular radius ~11 mas, physical radius 3.7×10^14 cm), and derives a recent dust mass-loss rate of 6.5×10^−8 M⊙/yr, consistent with the ~5–10×10^−8 M⊙/yr range implied by other analyses. The work highlights the value of high-resolution, broad-wavelength occultation data for probing circumstellar structure, while acknowledging limitations in flux calibration, spectral resolution, and the unresolved 60-µm discrepancy.",
